1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
ClearPath compares a scene with an aligned empty reference. This is a local development
recording of real CPU OpenCV five, using owned synthetic fixtures. AWS deployment and
eligibility remain pending.

2
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:32,000
The opposing-obstacles fixture has a measured two point four by six metre floor. The real
inspection reports a zero point seven five metre cross-section and one point six five
square metres of changed area.

3
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The overlays show the detected changes and calibrated floor projection. This number is the
smallest of each row's widest free segments. It does not prove a continuous route for a
person or wheelchair.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:04,000
I acknowledge reviewing the calibration, overlay, and limits, then download the actual
report. Review records an acknowledgement; it does not turn a synthetic result into a
safety certification.

5
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Now the out-of-focus capture. The quality gate requests another view instead of releasing
a measurement. The API returns no metrics, and the browser hides the old numbers.

6
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:38,000
Recapture is an actual decision from image evidence. The operator must supply a better
aligned image pair. This policy is deterministic, with no language model connected.

7
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:56,000
The low-contrast case exposes a failure. The detector misses the object and reports two
point four metres, even though the independent fixture label is one point four metres.

8
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:14,000
That is a one metre overestimate. The green result is misleading here. We retain this case
in evaluation and disclose it rather than claim general accuracy from ideal synthetic
fixtures.

9
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:30,000
The trace records actual OpenCV operations and review limits. The Linux container is
separately verified locally. Actual AWS processing, physical-capture validation, and
organizer eligibility confirmation remain pending.
